<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  entitlements.mac.plist — hardened runtime exceptions for StreamMagpie.

  electron-builder.yml passes this file as BOTH `mac.entitlements` (the outer
  .app) and `mac.entitlementsInherit` (every nested helper, and the four loose
  sidecar executables named under `mac.binaries`). Hardened Runtime is mandatory
  for notarization, and it switches off several things a Chromium app needs.

  ENTITLEMENTS ARE A SIGNING INPUT, not a runtime config file. codesign embeds
  this blob into the signature, so an edit here takes effect at the NEXT build
  and never on an already signed app; and a malformed file fails the build
  rather than degrading at runtime.

  Exactly two keys, and both are Electron's rather than StreamMagpie's:

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      V8 compiles JavaScript to native code at runtime and needs MAP_JIT pages
      to do it. Denied, Hardened Runtime refuses the mapping and Electron does
      not start at all. Concrete need: this is a Chromium app.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      Parts of Chromium create executable memory outside the MAP_JIT mechanism
      that allow-jit covers, which Hardened Runtime's W^X policy forbids on its
      own. Taken on Electron's documented say so rather than derived from first
      principles. Symptom if wrongly removed: crash at launch.

  THE RULE FOR THIS FILE: every key is a security exception, so it must name a
  concrete thing in THIS app that needs it. "the signing tool's default set
  includes it" is not a justification, because that set is written for the union
  of all Electron apps. Keys that are deliberately ABSENT are recorded with
  their evidence below the closing plist tag; read that before re-adding one.

  ⚠ NEVER WRITE A DOUBLE HYPHEN INSIDE ANY COMMENT ABOVE THE CLOSING PLIST TAG,
  not even inside backticks, and not even when quoting a command line flag. XML
  forbids it in a comment, and Apple's AMFI parser is the one tool in the chain
  that enforces it. The full account, the working notes on what this app
  actually bundles, and anything that has to quote a flag, all live BELOW the
  closing tag: the only region AMFI does not read.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
<!--
  ==========================================================================
  THE DOUBLE HYPHEN TRAP — read before editing any comment above </plist>
  ==========================================================================

  Symptom, and it blocks the entire signed build:

      codesign: Failed to parse entitlements:
                AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 37

  Cause: a literal `--` inside an XML comment. XML forbids it, and AMFI's
  parser is the only tool in the chain that enforces it — quoting a command
  line flag in backticks is enough to do it. The line number in the error is
  accurate; go straight there.

  ⚠ plutil -lint CANNOT CATCH THIS. It passes the broken file, because it
  discards comments before validating. Only a real codesign sees it.

  TWO THINGS THAT ARE PLAUSIBLE AND WRONG:

    - It is NOT "AMFI cannot handle comments inside the <plist> element".
      Per-key comments in <dict> parse fine; they are simply not worth the
      risk here, which is why the two keys above are documented in the header
      instead.

    - Moving comments ABOVE <plist version="1.0"> does NOT fix it. AMFI parses
      that region too. ONLY content after </plist> is ignored -- which is why
      this block can quote flags like --force and --deep freely, and the
      header cannot.

    - Non-ASCII is a red herring: box rules, em dashes and the warning sign
      all parse fine inside <dict>.

  TEST THIS FILE IN ONE SECOND, instead of a full packaging round trip. AMFI
  parses the entitlements blob whatever is being signed, so sign a throwaway
  copy of any binary with it. Note the PREFIXED identity form here: codesign
  wants the full certificate common name, unlike CSC_NAME in the build, which
  must NOT carry the prefix (see electron-builder.yml):

      cp /bin/echo /tmp/probe && codesign --force --options runtime \
        --entitlements packages/app/build/entitlements.mac.plist \
        --sign "Developer ID Application: Intheday Ltd (29UYFH4USR)" /tmp/probe

  Silence and exit 0 means this file is good. Add `-d --entitlements -` on the
  probe afterwards to see exactly which keys got embedded.

  RUN AGAINST A REAL CODESIGN, AND IT PASSED. The recipe above was executed
  verbatim on 2026-08-23 against the real Developer ID: exit 0, silent, no AMFI
  fault. `codesign -d --entitlements -` on the probe afterwards printed exactly
  two keys, allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory, and nothing else.
  The full signed build that followed raised no entitlements error either.
  [verified: run 2026-08-23]

  ⚠ THAT RESULT DOES NOT RETIRE THE TRAP. The fault is a property of whatever
  comment text this file carries TODAY, so every edit re-earns it. The static
  guard in `test/app-paths.test.ts` ("entitlements comments above plist have no
  double hyphen") still runs on every gate, and it is a string assertion doing a
  parser's job on purpose. Run the probe again after any edit above the closing
  tag: one second, against a nine-minute build.

  ==========================================================================
  WHAT THIS APP ACTUALLY BUNDLES — the thing the questions below turned on
  ==========================================================================

  StreamMagpie's payload is THIRD-PARTY SIDECARS, not code we compiled. Under
  `Contents/Resources`, from `extraResources` in electron-builder.yml:

    python/    a full CPython 3.12.14 runtime from python-build-standalone.
               That is `python/bin/python3` PLUS a `python/lib/` tree carrying
               exactly TEN further Mach-O images: `libpython3.12.dylib`, six
               Tcl/Tk dylibs (libtcl9.0, libtcl9tk9.0, libitcl4.3.8,
               libtcl9itcl4.3.8, libthread3.0.6, libtcl9thread3.0.6) and THREE
               `lib-dynload/*.so` extension modules (_crypt, _dbm, _tkinter).
               Ten, not hundreds, and not the ones this file used to name:
               _ssl, _socket, _hashlib and binascii are STATICALLY LINKED into
               libpython in this distribution and are not separate files at all.
               [verified: run 2026-08-23, counted in the shipped bundle]
    ffmpeg/    static LGPL `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe` built by scripts/build-ffmpeg.sh
    js/        `qjs`, the QuickJS interpreter yt-dlp drives for n-sig
    ytdlp/     the floor `yt_dlp.whl`. Data, not Mach-O. Not a signing concern.

  `mac.binaries` names only the four loose executables: ffmpeg, ffprobe, js/qjs,
  python/bin/python3. It is a LIST, and what it guarantees is that those four are
  signed WITH THE ENTITLEMENTS ABOVE, by name.

  ⚠ IT IS NOT THE ONLY THING THAT SIGNS, AND THIS FILE USED TO CLAIM IT WAS.
  The old claim was that anything not on the list ships unsigned, carrying
  whatever ad-hoc signature the upstream distribution gave it. FALSE for
  electron-builder 26.15.3. The build log shows the list handled first, as
  `signing additional user-defined binaries`, and then a SEPARATE
  `signing file=release/mac-arm64/StreamMagpie.app` step that walks the finished
  bundle recursively. All 14 Mach-O images in the shipped app came out with
  `Authority=Developer ID Application: Intheday Ltd (29UYFH4USR)`,
  `TeamIdentifier=29UYFH4USR` and `flags=0x10000(runtime)` — the ten under
  `Contents/Resources/python/lib/` that the list never names included.
  [verified: run 2026-08-23]

  That is not a footnote. The false belief was the entire basis of both
  questions below, and of a deep-sign pass they prescribed that turns out to be
  work electron-builder already does.

  ⚠ DO NOT DELETE `mac.binaries` ON THE STRENGTH OF THIS. It is what pins these
  entitlements onto those four by name, and whether removing it would change any
  of the 14 signatures has not been tested. [inferred]

  ==========================================================================
  TWO QUESTIONS, BOTH ANSWERED BY THE FIRST SIGNED BUILD — 2026-08-23
  ==========================================================================

  Both were honest unknowns, and both rested on the claim corrected above.
  Neither could be resolved by reading; the first signed, notarized and stapled
  build resolved both, and the answer to each is no.

  (a) DOES NOTARIZATION REJECT THE `.so` TREE? NO — and NOT because Apple was
      lenient. That distinction is the whole finding. Apple's tolerance was
      never tested, because there was nothing unsigned left to object to:
      electron-builder's recursive pass over the finished `.app` had already
      signed every nested image with our Developer ID under the hardened
      runtime, so the submission carried no foreign-team and no ad-hoc Mach-O
      at all. The build logged `notarization successful` once, the `.app` came
      back reporting `Notarization Ticket=stapled`, and
      `codesign --verify --deep --strict` called it "valid on disk" and said it
      "satisfies its Designated Requirement". [verified: run 2026-08-23]

      The deep-sign pass this section used to prescribe is therefore NOT needed.
      It was the right fix for the problem as understood; the problem does not
      exist.

  (b) DOES LIBRARY VALIDATION KILL CPython's `dlopen`? NO, and this was tested
      directly rather than inferred from (a). Library validation requires that
      any code loaded INTO a process be signed by the same Team ID as the
      process, or by Apple. `python3` dlopens its C-extension modules at import
      time, which is precisely the operation that rule governs. So the imports
      were run, from the app installed into /Applications, under the hardened
      runtime it shipped with, with this file still at two keys:

          python3 --version
              Python 3.12.14, exit 0

          python3 -c 'import ssl, zlib, ctypes, sqlite3, hashlib, bz2, lzma,
                      socket, binascii'
              all C extensions imported OK, exit 0

          PYTHONPATH=<floor wheel> python3 -c 'import yt_dlp'
              yt_dlp 2026.08.19 loaded, exit 0

      No SIGKILL, no empty log, no dialog. Same team throughout, so both
      branches of the rule are satisfied and no exception is needed.
      [verified: run 2026-08-23]

      Note the process that matters is `python3` itself, spawned as a CHILD:
      a child carries its own signature, and library validation on the Electron
      parent has no opinion about it. Any entitlement would therefore have had
      to reach python3 via `entitlementsInherit`, which is this same file. It
      did not need to.

      ⚠ KEEP THE SYMPTOM ON RECORD ANYWAY. It is the least diagnosable failure
      in this class, and a payload change can reintroduce it. If library
      validation ever rejects an extension module, the extractor dies by INSTANT
      SIGKILL the moment a conversion starts: no error, no dialog, nothing in
      any log. AN EMPTY LOG IS THE SIGNATURE OF THIS FAILURE. A conversion that
      "does nothing" on a signed build, and works from source, is this until
      proven otherwise.

      Do not add the key speculatively to make a SIGKILL go away. With it set,
      ANY dylib can be loaded into the process, which is a real widening of the
      attack surface. Establish WHAT got rejected first: `codesign -dv --deep`
      over `Contents/Resources/python` and `log stream` around the spawn.

  ⚠ THIS FILE HAS NOW BEEN WRONG TWICE ABOUT THE SAME KEY, IN OPPOSITE
  DIRECTIONS, AND BOTH WRONG TURNS ARE WORTH KEEPING. The earliest version
  argued disable-library-validation was safely absent because the sidecar was
  "a single executable that WE build and the signer signs under OUR identity",
  linking nothing outside /usr/lib and /System. Right conclusion, but from a
  description of a DIFFERENT app: StreamMagpie bundles a third-party
  interpreter, not a self-built sidecar. The version that replaced it fixed the
  payload description and then overcorrected, reopening the question on the
  strength of the "mac.binaries is the only thing that signs" claim that the
  first real build disproved. The absence is settled again, and this time on
  observed reasons rather than reasoned ones: everything in the bundle is
  signed, it is all one team, and the imports were actually run.

  ==========================================================================
  DELIBERATELY ABSENT — recorded with evidence so nobody re-adds them blindly
  ==========================================================================

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — ABSENT, and no longer
    provisionally so. The bundled interpreter imports its C extensions under the
    hardened runtime it shipped with, from the app installed in /Applications,
    with this key not present: `import ssl, zlib, ctypes, sqlite3, hashlib, bz2,
    lzma, socket, binascii` exits 0, and so does `import yt_dlp` off the floor
    wheel. Library validation is satisfied because electron-builder signs the
    whole nested tree under our own Team ID. See (b) above.
    [verified: run 2026-08-23]

    ⚠ Still do not add it speculatively. With it set, ANY dylib can be loaded
    into the process. That evidence is about THIS payload on THIS build, not a
    standing guarantee: if a future payload arrives pre-signed by another team,
    or a SIGKILL with an empty log appears, establish what was rejected first.

  com.apple.security.app-sandbox — ABSENT, and it never gets added.

    StreamMagpie spawns bundled executables (python3, ffmpeg, qjs) and writes
    audio into a folder the user picks. A sandbox would need a temporary
    exception for each of those, and sandboxed processes may not spawn
    arbitrary bundled binaries at all.

    ⚠ THIS FORECLOSES THE MAC APP STORE, permanently and knowingly. The App
    Store requires the sandbox, and would not accept a YouTube extractor in any
    case. That is fine: the official build is sold once through Gumroad
    (docs/DISTRIBUTION.md) and the source is MIT for anyone who would rather
    build it.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — ABSENT.

    The plausible and WRONG argument for it: "the main process sets environment
    variables before spawning children, and Hardened Runtime strips those unless
    this is set". `src/main/helper-paths.ts` does exactly that, setting
    STREAMMAGPIE_PYTHON, STREAMMAGPIE_FFMPEG, STREAMMAGPIE_QJS and friends as an
    import-time side effect, which the engine reads when it spawns.

    The entitlement governs `DYLD_*` variables ONLY (DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES,
    DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH and the rest) — the dynamic-loader ones dyld ignores under
    Hardened Runtime precisely because they are a code injection vector.
    STREAMMAGPIE_* are ordinary environment variables. Ordinary variables are
    untouched and propagate to child processes normally.

    Checked: `grep -rn "DYLD_" packages/*/src scripts/` returns nothing.
    [verified: run]

  com.apple.security.network.client — ABSENT, and NOT because the app is
    offline: it plainly is not. `src/main/extractor-update.ts` fetches the
    signed extractor manifest, and yt-dlp reaches YouTube for every conversion.
    This key is a SANDBOX entitlement. An unsandboxed process already has
    unrestricted outbound network access, so the key grants nothing and would
    only matter if the sandbox above were ever adopted, which it is not.

  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — ABSENT.

    "Play" and "Show in Finder" go through Electron's `shell.openPath` and
    `shell.showItemInFolder`, which are LaunchServices asking the OS to open a
    path. Neither sends an Apple event, so neither needs this entitlement and
    neither raises a TCC automation prompt.

  ==========================================================================
  ONE MORE MECHANISM, OFTEN CONFUSED WITH THIS FILE
  ==========================================================================

  The NS*UsageDescription strings in `electron-builder.yml` are NOT
  entitlements. They are per-folder TCC prompt copy (Downloads, Desktop,
  Documents) that macOS shows when the app first writes into one of those
  folders, and they fire whether or not any entitlement is present. A missing
  string there is a silent EPERM at save time, which the app would surface as a
  conversion that finishes and produces no file.

  Likewise, the TCC identity every one of those grants attaches to is the
  bundle identifier `co.streammagpie.app`. No plist key requests a TCC grant and
  no build step can. Change that string and every user's existing grants are
  orphaned onto an app that no longer exists, which is why AGENTS.md refuses to
  let it be renamed.
-->
